laurel 2710: one SPMI core for every SPMI user (phantom key presses every 10 s). Boot 2026-11-01 06:17 (nobody at the phone): `SWEET KEYS: key=0 down=1 pon=0x7f` + key=2 at 06:19:17/27/37 (every 10 s = the charger guard timer / battery poll), vol-up `gpio4:c110=0x78/0x28/0x18` (bit 0 low = pressed) at :x5 every 10 s, and `SWEET KEYS: read error ... arb-status=0x9` (STATUS DONE + DROPPED). Cause: three SPMI users each had their own path to the same arbiter v5: sweet_keys.c (own map, no lock, polls PON APID 112 + GPIO5 APID 124 every 20 ms), sweet_spmi.c (IOLock for the battery; the PON and USBIN lanes without a lock) and laurel_haptic.c (own map, own mutex; "each APID has its own command registers, so the lanes do not race"). Two commands at the same time on two observer channels of EE 0 were dropped (0x9) or completed with the other command's RDATA (pon/gpio values that are battery data), and the keys thread took one bad read as a press. Linux spmi-pmic-arb.c holds one lock (pmic_arb->lock, raw spinlock, irqsave) over every transaction. New: sweet_spmi.c is the one core: one channel map (PPID -> APID, owner EE, read once at init, under a mutex), one transaction lock (LDXR/STXR word, interrupts off, held from the command store to the RDATA read; power off / restart / panic hold users run after a 5 ms wait without it, counted as forced), DONE wait 1 ms, FAILURE/DENIED/DROPPED = error, timeout poisons the APID. sweet_keys.c, laurel_haptic.c, the PON lane (laurel_reboot.c, laurel_pmi632.c), the USBIN lane and sweet_spmi_read/_write (battery, charger, ADC, SDAM, thermal) all use it. Keys: a key changes only after 2 agreeing valid polls (20 ms apart); a failed read never changes a key. Lines: `LAUREL SPMI: on: arbiter 0x50010000, 256 APIDs, one lock and one channel map for every transaction; users: pon keys battery haptic; spmi errors pon 0 keys 0 battery 0 haptic 0, forced 0` (60 s after the first SPMI init), the 5-min `LAUREL BATTERY: on: ...; spmi errors ...`, `LAUREL 